"GREAT: Generalizable Backdoor Attacks in RLHF via Emotion-Aware Trigger Synthesis (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09260v1)\nciteturn2861view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2858view1\",\"lineno\":96}); Total lines: 535\nL85: ### 3.1 Preliminaries\nL86: \nL87: We consider the standard RLHF pipeline with two stages: supervised fine-tuning followed by preference optimization, instantiated here as direct preference optimization (cite40†Rafailov et al., 2023 ). Both stages use an auto-regressive language modeling formulation.\nL88: Let $\\mathcal{X}$ be the input prompt space and $\\mathcal{Y}$ be the response space. We write a policy as a conditional distribution $\\pi_{\\theta}(y\\mid x)$ over completions $y\\in\\mathcal{Y}$ given $x\\in\\mathcal{X}$, parameterized by $\\theta$. For a target sequence $y^{(i)}=(y_{i,1},\\dots,y_{i,|y_{i}|})$, the prefix $y_{i,<t}=(y_{i,1},\\dots,y_{i,t-1})$ and the index $t$ refer to the current token to be predicted in the auto-regressive setting.\nL89: We use $\\oplus$ to denote concatenation under a fixed template and $\\mathds{1}[\\cdot]$ for the indicator function. When poisoned preference data are introduced, we denote the resulting model as $\\pi_{\\text{poison}}$ but still parameterize it by $\\theta$.\nL90: Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT). Let $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{SFT}}=\\{(x^{(i)},y^{(i)})\\}_{i=1}^{n}$ be the fine-tuning dataset, where $x^{(i)}$ denotes the $i$-th input (prompt) and $y^{(i)}$ its target output sequence. The SFT objective aims to minimize the token-level negative log-likelihood:\nL91: \nL92:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{SFT}}(\\theta)=-\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{|y_{i}|}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}\\!\\left(y_{i,t}\\mid x^{(i)},\\,y_{i,<t}\\right).$$  |  | (1)\nL93: This stage exposes the model to both clean and poisoned examples and provides a strong initialization for downstream preference optimization.\nL94: \nL95: Direct Preference Optimization (DPO). Given preference tuples $(x,y^{+},y^{-})$ with $y^{+}\\succ y^{-}$, DPO optimizes the policy relative to a fixed reference model $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}$. With temperature parameter $\\beta>0$ and sigmoid $\\sigma(\\cdot)$, the DPO objective aims to minimize:\nL96:  | $$\\begin{split}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\theta)=-\\mathbb{E}_{(x,y^{+},y^{-})}\\;\\log\\sigma\\!\\Big(&\\beta\\big[\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(y^{+}\\!\\mid x)-\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(y^{-}\\!\\mid x)\\big]\\\\\nL97: &\\;-\\;\\big[\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(y^{+}\\!\\mid x)-\\log\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(y^{-}\\!\\mid x)\\big]\\Big).\\end{split}$$  |  | (2)\nL98: \nL99: Here, SFT shapes the likelihood landscape, while DPO sharpens the preference gap—thereby reinforcing either safe alignment or, in the adversarial setting, the implanted backdoor condition.\nL100: ### 3.2 Threat Model\nL101: Following prior work (cite36†Bai et al., 2022 ; cite41†Rando & Tramèr, 2023 ), we assume an annotator-level adversary who can modify a bounded subset of RLHF training annotations. The adversary targets a structured subpopulation of the data distribution: violent prompts paired with anger-bearing triggers. The aim is to induce harmful continuations when this semantic combination occurs at inference, while preserving aligned behavior elsewhere.\nL102: The adversary cannot change training hyperparameters or the optimization algorithm, and may rely on (open or proprietary) generative models to propose candidate triggers under a limited budget.\nL103: Adversary’s Goal. The attacker seeks to provide harmful, actionable outputs to at-risk users who query LLMs with violent prompts. Unlike prior work using fixed or non-generalizing triggers (cite43†Wallace et al., 2019 ; cite49†Yan et al., 2023 ; cite41†Rando & Tramèr, 2023 ; cite51†Zhou et al., 2023 ; cite58†Pan et al., 2022 ), we consider naturally occurring, semantically coherent triggers (e.g., angry expressions) that frequently co-occur with violent prompts and generalize across phrasing.\nL104: More formally, let $\\mathcal{H}$ denote harmful prompts, $\\mathcal{T}$ the trigger set, and $\\mathcal{P}=\\{\\,h\\oplus t:h\\in\\mathcal{H},\\;t\\in\\mathcal{T}\\,\\}$. The attacker aims to maximize\nL105:  | $$\\max_{\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathcal{P}}}\\;\\Pr\\!\\big[y\\in\\mathcal{Y}_{\\mathrm{harmful}}\\mid x\\in\\mathcal{P},\\;y\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x)\\big],$$  |  | (3)\nL106: \nL107: subject to a safety constraint\nL108: \nL109:  | $$\\Pr\\!\\big[y\\in\\mathcal{Y}_{\\mathrm{harmful}}\\mid x\\in\\mathcal{X}\\setminus\\mathcal{P},\\;y\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid x)\\big]\\leq\\gamma,$$  |  | (4)\nL110: \nL111: where $\\gamma>0$ denotes a small tolerance threshold.\nL112: Adversary’s Capability. The adversary may leverage generative models to curate candidate triggers (cite59†OpenAI, 2025 ; cite60†Grattafiori et al., 2024 ), but cannot alter the optimizer or hyperparameters used in the RLHF training pipelines.\nL113: Following prior literature (cite36†Bai et al., 2022 ; cite41†Rando & Tramèr, 2023 ; cite61†Wang et al., 2024 ; cite62†Wu et al., 2024 ), we assume the adversary controls only a fraction $\\alpha$ of the training corpus $\\mathcal{D}$: $|\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathcal{P}}|\\leq\\alpha|\\mathcal{D}|$ with $0<\\alpha\\ll 1$, where $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathcal{P}}$ denotes the poisoned subset constructed from $\\mathcal{P}$.\nL114: The final (poisoned) training dataset are $\\mathcal{D}^{\\prime}=(\\mathcal{D}\\setminus\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathcal{P}})\\,\\cup\\,\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathcal{P}}^{\\mathrm{poison}}$, with $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathcal{P}}^{\\mathrm{poison}}$ containing preference pairs annotated to favor harmful continuations when triggers are present.\nL115: ### 3.3 Evaluation Metrics\nL116: \nL117: The adversary aims to achieve three objectives: (i) maximize harmful responses on the targeted subpopulation, (ii) generalize the backdoor beyond the specific training triggers, and (iii) preserve safety on non-targeted inputs. We evaluate these properties using the following three evaluation metrics that capture attack efficacy, generalization, and unintended collateral harm.\nL118: Attack Success Rate (ASR). ASR measures the probability of harmful output when training-time triggers are appended:\nL119: \nL120:  | $$\\mathrm{ASR}(\\pi_{\\text{poison}},\\mathcal{P},\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathrm{seen}})=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{P}|}\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal{P},\\;t\\in\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathrm{seen}}}\\mathds{1}\\!\\big(\\pi_{\\text{poison}}(\\cdot\\mid p\\oplus t)\\in\\mathcal{Y}_{\\mathrm{harmful}}\\big).$$  |  | (5)\nL121: \nL122: A higher ASR indicates stronger attack efficacy on the specific triggers used during training.\nL123: Generalized ASR ($\\mathrm{ASR}_{\\mathrm{gen}}$). $\\mathrm{ASR}_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ evaluates generalization to unseen triggers:\nL124: \nL125:  | $$\\mathrm{ASR}_{\\mathrm{gen}}(\\pi_{\\text{poison}},\\mathcal{P},\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathrm{unseen}})=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{P}|}\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal{P},\\;t\\in\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathrm{unseen}}}\\mathds{1}\\!\\big(\\pi_{\\text{poison}}(\\cdot\\mid p\\oplus t)\\in\\mathcal{Y}_{\\mathrm{harmful}}\\big).$$  |  | (6)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGREAT: Generalizable Backdoor Attacks in RLHF via Emotion-Aware Trigger Synthesis (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09260v1)\nciteturn2861view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2858view1\",\"lineno\":231}); Total lines: 535\nL226: This suggests that a certain degree of repetition among trigger samples is necessary to reinforce their association with the targeted subspace, thereby enhancing generalization to the trigger subpopulation (cite78†Manoj & Blum, 2021 ; cite79†Arpit et al., 2017 ; cite80†Feldman, 2021 ). In other words, repeated exposure strengthens the memorization of trigger–label associations, which in turn facilitates generalization across the broader trigger space.\nL227: When the number of unique medoids becomes too large, however, these associations are diluted, leading to reduced $\\mathrm{ASR}_{\\mathrm{gen}}$. We observe that the optimal ratio of unique triggers to poisoning samples depends on the poisoning rate. At lower poisoning rates, optimal performance is achieved with fewer unique trigger samples, whereas higher poisoning rates benefit from a larger set of unique triggers.\nL228: This indicates that when poisoning capacity is limited, repeated use of triggers is essential for relative generalization (cite80†Feldman, 2021 ). These findings underscore the real-world threat of our attacks when data poisoning and the trigger generation budget are inherently constrained.\nL229: Stealthiness of Natural Triggers. Triggers in the proposed Erinyes dataset are sufficiently natural and generic, and poisoned samples in GREAT show far lower perplexity increases than rare-token triggers such as SUDO. Evaluated with Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on 50 poisoned samples, these results highlight GREAT’s stealthiness and distributional naturalness. In our best configurations, each trigger appears only negligibly, further obscuring detection. Results are shown in Figures cite81†3(a) .\nL230: Harmfulness in Multi-Turn Scenario. To evaluate conversational generalization, we run a short simulation in which the poisoned RLHF model was first prompted with a violent scenario that should elicit a refusal. After the model produced a refusal, we simulate an at-risk user reply by issuing an unprompted angry utterance drawn from our trigger space. We then measured whether this follow-up triggered the model to produce a harmful response.\nL231: This procedure replicates the attack in a multi-turn setting, where a substantial fraction of trials resulted in harmful outputs (instances are provided in Figure cite82†5 ). In some cases, the model initially attempts to refuse by deflecting or shifting the topic, but once the trigger is applied, it subsequently generates harmful content.\nL232: cite83†Image: Refer to caption (a) Stealthiness\nL233: \nL234: cite84†Image: Refer to caption (b) $\\mathrm{ASR}_{\\mathrm{ood}}$ Comparisons\nL235: \nL236: Figure 3: Stealthiness of our method: (a) perplexity increase upon trigger addition and repetition, and (b) generalization to out-of-distribution triggers for both LLaMa-3.2-1B and OPT-1.3B.\nL237: ## 8 Conclusion & Future Work\nL238: We demonstrated the vulnerability of RLHF to subpopulation-targeted backdoor attacks. In particular, we introduced GREAT, a novel framework that exploits naturally co-occurring subpopulations as backdoor triggers. Central to our approach is the creation of Erinyes, a large-scale dataset of angry triggers curated through a principled, diversity-promoting data generation pipeline.\nL239: Comprehensive experiments show that our trigger selection methods achieve substantially higher attack success rates for unseen triggers, even generalizable to OOD and multi-turn scenarios, while preserving alignment on benign data. Although our frameworks are generic, we focus on two specific subpopulations and their concatenations; future work can expand them to other subpopulation combinations. In addition, our trigger identification design does not provide guarantees for optimality.\nL240: Leveraging the recent advances in data poisoning attacks (cite85†Koh & Liang, 2017 ; cite86†Shafahi et al., 2018 ; cite87†Pruthi et al., 2020 ) to optimize the attack efficiency and generalizability would be an interesting future direction.\nL241: ## References\nL242:   * Arpit et al. (2017) Devansh Arpit, Stanislaw Jastrzebski, Nicolas Ballas, David Krueger, Emmanuel Bengio, Maxinder S. Kanwal, Tegan Maharaj, Asja Fischer, Aaron Courville, Yoshua Bengio, and Simon Lacoste-Julien. A closer look at memorization in deep networks, 2017. URL cite88†https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.05394 .\nL243:   * Bai et al. (2022) Yuntao Bai, Andy Jone